@paulidale I am not sure what the problem is with getting a reference ?
You need a reference (a provider context) to access RAND_bytes as well.
So you just add a new function on the OSSL_OP_RAND table, and you check if it is populated or NULL. If it is populated you use it, if not libcrypto uses the default DRBG.

comments:
That's nowhere near the OpenSSL use case :grin:

Does the FIPS compliant CRNG include the CRNG tests of it's entropy source?  It's only necessary to test the entropy source, not the output of any whitening.  I.e. if the kernel's entropy source does the test, things will be FIPS compliant.  It might require a bit of hand waving and some lab beating.

If this isn't possible, force the parent of the primary DRBG to NULL via librcypto:

```diff
diff --git a/crypto/rand/rand_lib.c b/crypto/rand/rand_lib.c
index 14999540ab..b05b84717b 100644
--- a/crypto/rand/rand_lib.c
+++ b/crypto/rand/rand_lib.c
@@ -723,15 +723,7 @@ EVP_RAND_CTX *RAND_get0_primary(OSSL_LIB_CTX *ctx)
         return ret;
     }
 
-#ifndef FIPS_MODULE
-    if (dgbl->seed == NULL) {
-        ERR_set_mark();
-        dgbl->seed = rand_new_seed(ctx);
-        ERR_pop_to_mark();
-    }
-#endif
-
-    ret = dgbl->primary = rand_new_drbg(ctx, dgbl->seed,
+    ret = dgbl->primary = rand_new_drbg(ctx, NULL,
                                         PRIMARY_RESEED_INTERVAL,
                                         PRIMARY_RESEED_TIME_INTERVAL, 1);
     /*
```

This loses the ability to specify your own seed source though.
IMO, this is the only approach that will work without modifying the FIPS provider.

It could be made less draconian by checking the provider and only setting a NULL parent for the FIPS provider.  This isn't clean since it means fetching the DRBG twice -- once to determine the provider and a second time with the appropriate parent.  Fetching is also not guaranteed to return the same both times but in practice it would.
